/* book: lead: A multiplier is slow for two reasons: too many partial-product rows to add, or a long carry chain through them. A Wallace tree reduces the rows with parallel 3:2 counters, so the carry chain almost disappears until the last step. Booth recoding changes the multiplier into signed digits, so there are fewer rows to reduce. High-speed CPU and DSP multipliers use both ideas together. */
/* book Figure 4.4.1: A Wallace tree on eight partial-product rows. Each carry save adder turns three rows into two, so four levels leave two rows for the one final adder. */
