/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Start with N partial-product rows from Chapter 4.3.
   - At each level, split the rows into disjoint groups of three.
   - D is the number of reduction levels, N is the starting row count, and ⌈ ⌉ means round up.
   - After the last level two rows remain.
   - Booth radix-2 recodes the multiplier Q into signed digits.
   - Then Q = Σ di 2i.
   - Modified Booth radix-4 reads overlapping triplets and produces digits from −2 to +2.
   - The digit is di = −2q2i+1 + q2i + q2i−1, and its weight is 4i.
   - A radix-4 digit covers two multiplier bits, so an n-bit multiplier gives about n/2 digits.
*/
